# Line-ending policy for the Neurofebric harness.
#
# The repository stores every text file with LF endings. That was already true
# of every committed blob before this file existed -- verified by scanning
# origin/main for CR bytes and finding none -- so this file RECORDS the existing
# state and pins it. It does not change any byte already in the repository, and
# `git add --renormalize .` over this tree is a no-op. That no-op is the
# evidence that the policy matches reality rather than a migration applied over
# it.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text by content and normalize on check-in.
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree as well, so a checkout on Windows
# stops producing CRLF. That matters here: this harness is developed on Windows
# and gated on linux runners, and a CRLF checkout is a class of spurious diff
# that a mixed-platform team will eventually commit by accident.
#
# The Windows carve-out is deliberately absent. No tracked file is a .bat, .cmd
# or .ps1 -- 213 tracked files, zero of them -- so the carve-out protects
# nothing that exists today. It is written anyway, FORWARD-LOOKING ONLY: a
# Windows-native script with CRLF endings is correct, and the first person to
# add one should not have to rediscover that `text=auto` will rewrite it.
#
# D-028 -- the CI gate that consumes this checkout.
# D-024  -- the terminal-state discipline for a change with no runtime effect.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
